The genealogy of Jesus.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham begat Isaac. Isaac begat Jacob. Jacob begat Judah and his brothers.
Judah was my father, and Perez and Zerah were his sons. Their mother was Tamar. Perez was my father, and Hezron. Hezron was my father, and Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Boaz was the father of Perez. Rahab was the mother of Perez. Perez was the father of Hezron. Ruth was the mother of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Aram.
And Jesse the father of king David. And David the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon Abu Ribai. Ribai Abu Abia. Abia Abu Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jonathan and his brothers. And in that time, the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Yehonya was the father of Shaltiel. Shaltiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azur was a father to Zadu. Zadu was a father to Akim. Akim was a father to Eliud.
Elmod was a father of Eleazar. Eleazar was a father of Mathan. Mathan was a father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. There were 14 generations from David to the time when the Jews were forced to migrate to Babylon. There were 14 generations from that time to Christ. The birth of Jesus Christ
Thus Jesus Christ was born. The mother of Jesus, Mary, and her husband, Joseph, had promised to marry. But before they began living together, it became clear that she was pregnant. She conceived through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusef, was a righteous man. He did not want to shame her publicly. So, he planned for a quiet divorce.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary your wife into your home, for the child who is conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You shall name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All of this was to fulfill what the Lord had said would happen. The Lord said through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive and bear a son. She will bring forth a boy, and he will be called Immanuel.
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not consummate their marriage until she had given birth to a son. And Joseph called the child Jesus.
Two wise women visit Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened in the time of Herod the king. After the birth of Jesus, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be the king of the Jews? For we saw his star when we were in the east, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and it distressed everyone in Jerusalem as well.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they replied. "For this is what the prophet has written: 'The Lord was pleased to come from his dwelling place in heaven to live on earth. He chose a town in Judea, the small and insignificant town of Bethlehem. From this town he chose to come, the one who is to be our ruler, the one foretold by the prophets of old.'"
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come for me one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. When he had called them in secret, he inquired of them where the Christ was born.
Send them to Bethlehem. And he said, "Go! Search carefully for the child. As soon as you find him, bring me a report. Then I can go worship him too."
After the sage had listened to the king, they set out on their way. And the star that they had seen when they were in the East preceded them, until it came and stood over where the child was.
When they saw the star, it filled them with joy.
The sage went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another route. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
When the Magi had departed, Joseph had a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Rise!" said the angel. "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod will be seeking the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph awoke. And on that night he went out to Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until Herod died. Thus the word of the Lord spoken through the prophet was fulfilled: "I have indeed planned to bring my son out of Egypt" (Jeremiah 11:1).
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He gave orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the male children who were two years old and under were killed. This was in accordance with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
And in this way the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of lamentation and deep mourning. Rachel weeps for her children, refusing to be comforted because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while still in Egypt. And in the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Arise, take the infant and his mother, and go to the land of Israel, for those who sought to kill the infant are dead."
Then Joseph arose, and took the newborn child and his mother Mary, and brought them up into the land of Israel.
But when he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea, Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod, Joseph became afraid to go there. Joseph was warned in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a town called Nazareth. And so what the prophets had said about Jesus came true. They had said: "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist paves the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said: "Desist from your sins. The angel of death is nearby."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice is calling out, 'In the wilderness, prepare the way of the Lord, and make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to him from Jerusalem and all Judah, and also from all the region around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the River Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. And he said to them, "You brood of vipers! How can you escape the condemnation of hell? For I tell you, unless you repent and turn from your evil ways, you will all perish and be eternally lost."
Produce fruit in which it is seen that you have abandoned your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The shovel is already at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water for repentance, but he who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry; he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
Jesus said, "The Son of Man is like a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field. It grew and became a large tree, and the birds of the air made nests in its branches."
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. He said to Jesus, "I must baptize you. So why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered: "Leave her alone, as it is fitting to do so. She is fulfilling the divine and sacred plan." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. At that moment the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God come down on him like a dove.
And a voice came from heaven, saying, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. There the devil tested him.
After 40 days and 40 nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
The tempter came to him. And he said, "If you are the Son of God, command that these stones become loaves of bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. And he stood him on the top of the highest pinnacle of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down, for it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, to guard you carefully; they will carry you in their arms, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus answered, saying, "It is written, 'Do not test the Lord your God'" (Deuteronomy 6:16).
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you fall prostrate and worship me," he said, "then I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.' " (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil came out of Jesus. Angels came and helped him. Jesus begins to be great.
John was put in prison. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the town of Capernaum. It was by the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And in this way, I have made good what the prophet Isaiah had said. For he had said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! Land along the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
The people who are now living in darkness, will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them.
From that time, Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus Chooses the First Disciples
One day Jesus was walking along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw two brothers, they were fishermen, one was called Simon, who is also known as Peter, and the other Andrew his brother, they were casting their net into the lake.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
And from there, he saw two other brothers. They were James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And as they were preparing two fishing nets, Jesus called out to them.
When they heard about him, they left everything, including the boats and their father, and followed Jesus. Jesus heals sick people.
Jesus traveled throughout Galilee. And there he taught in the synagogues. And he taught the gospel of the kingdom of God. And he healed every disease and every weakness of the people.
The news about him spread everywhere in Syria. People came to him from everywhere, bringing with them all kinds of sick people. Some of them were suffering from severe pain. Others were possessed by demons. Some of them were shaking violently. Others could not move at all. Jesus healed them all.
A great multitude followed him. Some people came from Galilee, and from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. And others came from the region located across the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds, so he went up on a mountain and lay down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are the needy, for to them belongs the Kingdom of the Heavens.
Blessed are those who are grieved, for they shall be comforted.
Blessed are the meek. They shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are the hungry and thirsty for righteousness, for they shall be filled.
Blessed is the most merciful of the merciful. Those who show mercy to others will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the people of pure hearts, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers. For they shall be called the children of God.
Blessed are the doers of works, by which they will be tormented. For them is the kingdom of the heavens.
Blessed are you when people revile you and persecute you on my account. Blessed are you when people say all manner of evil against you on my account.
Rejoice and make others rejoice. Truly, your reward is with God, the Great. Similarly, people have harmed the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can you make it salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out and trampled on.
You are the light of the world. A city on a hill cannot be hidden.
Also, people don't light a lamp and put it under a basket. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then it illuminates everyone in the house.
Jesus gives the law its full meaning.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish, but to complete.
Truly I tell you, until heaven and earth disappear, not the smallest mark or stroke of a pen will disappear from the Law until everything is accomplished.
Do not deny one of the least of these commandments. Nor teach others to break them. For if you do, you will be called the least important of people in the Kingdom of Heaven. But practice and teach these commandments. Then you will be called important people in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
You have heard what was said to the people who lived a long time ago. It was said to them, "You shall not murder. (Exodus 20:13) Whoever murders will be put to death."
But here is what I say. Do not be angry with your brother. Everyone who is angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. Again, whoever says to his brother, 'You good-for-nothing!' must be put on trial in the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, 'You good-for-nothing!' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Assume that you are offering your gift at the altar. And remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Assume someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do so while you are still with him on the road. If you do not do that, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am going to tell you is true. You are not leaving until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But here is what I say. Do not even look at a woman with lust. Whoever looks at her with lust has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. For your eye is only a part of your body. It is better for you to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. For it is not your hand that sins, but your whole body that is in danger of being thrown into hell. Divorce.
They said, 'O Moses, indeed some of the people take their women unlawfully, without knowledge. And if they are truthful to them, then they do not find in themselves anything [of regret] to marry them, even if they are truthful to them with a dowry of ten loads. Then
But here is what I say. Anyone who divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery. And anyone who marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man should not divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him. Oath.
And again, you have heard what was said to his people long ago. They were told, "Do not deny the covenants you make with the Lord your God. You must carry out the terms of the covenant you have made with him."
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't make any such promise at all. Don't make it, for heaven's sake. It's God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the land, for it is the pillow of the feet of God; and do not make it in the name of Jerusalem, for it is the city of the great King.
Do not swear by your heads, you cannot make a single white or black hair.
Just let "yes" mean "yes." And let "no" mean "no." Anything more than this comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth" (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here's what I say. Don't fight an evil person. "Suppose someone strikes you on your right cheek. Offer him your left cheek also.
If someone takes you to court to take your shirt, let them take your coat as well.
Assume someone forces you to walk one mile. Go with him two miles.
Give to the one who asks you for something. Do not hate the one who employs you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, 'Love your neighbor.' (Deuteronomy 19:18) 'Hate your enemy.'
But here is what I say. Love your enemies. Pray for those who mistreat you.
"Then you will be the children of your Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love those who love you, what reward do you have? Even the tax collectors do that.
If you are only greeting your people, what more are you doing than others? Even non-believers do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to the poor.
Beware of doing righteous works before people so that they may see you. Do not do them so that they may see. For your Father who is in heaven does not reward those who act secretly.
If you spend, do not follow it up with harm or by reminding of your gift, like one who spends his wealth to be seen by people, and does not believe in God or the Last Day. So their example is as the example of a dog that pants whether you strike it or leave it alone. Such is the parable of those who deny Our signs. Tell those who disbelieve that if they cease, what has happened in the past is forgiven for them; but if they return to it, then the example of the consequence for their deeds has already occurred. And tell those who believe to forgive those who hope for the meeting with Us, and who avert evil with good. For those, the recompense is with their Lord; for those, forgiveness and noble wages are due. It is only those who believe in Our signs and are Muslims that are your brothers. And for those who harm you, retaliate against them to the same degree that they harmed you. But if you are patient and fear Allah, then you will not be afflicted by any harm from them. And if any harm comes to you from anything that has been created by Allah, then it is an act of aggression by them. And in such a case, whoever is patient and forgives, then that is an act of great determination. And if anyone does evil or wrongs his soul and then seeks pardon from Allah, he will find Allah Forgiving and Merciful. And whoever commits a sin only injures himself. And Allah is Knowing and Wise. And whoever commits a sin only injures himself. And Allah is Knowing and Wise. And whoever commits a sin only injures himself. And Allah is Knowing and Wise. And whoever commits a sin only injures himself. And Allah is Knowing and Wise
When you give to the poor, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your charity will be secret. Your father rewards you. He sees what you do in your secrets. Prayer.
إِلَّا مَوَاضِعَ قَلِيلَةً ۚ وَلَا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ تَقْلِيدًا ۚ وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ مَا تَقْلِيدُهُمْ ۗ بَلْ هُمْ قَوْمٌ يُسَارُ عَلَيْهِمْ ۗ وَإِنْ كَانُوا لَا يُعْرَفُونَ ۚ ثُمَّ صَلُّوا صَلَاتَهُمْ وَ
If you perform the prayer, then enter the mosques of God - indeed, God loves those who fear Him. Indeed, God is near, responding. He knows what you do.
"If you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, who say to their brothers, who were associating with them in secret, 'Indeed, I am with you. Indeed, they are only saying what they are saying based on their assumption. They do not understand."
Do not be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
Thus you should pray. 'Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Do what you want to happen on earth as it happens in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our sins, even as we forgive those who trespass against us.
"Deliver us from falling into sin when we are tempted. And save us from the accursed Satan."
Forgive others their sins, and if you do that, your father who is in heaven will forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
If you fast, do not be like the hypocrites. Those are the ones who deceive themselves. They have certainly made clear what they should taste, and Allah has certainly turned to them in repentance. Indeed, Allah is Informed of what they do.
But if you are not fasting, anoint your heads with oil and wash your faces.
Then no one knows that you are fasting, except your father, who is in heaven. Your father, who is in heaven, sees what you are doing. Your father, who is in heaven, sees what you are doing. Then your father, who is in heaven, will reward you.
Do not hide your wealth in the earth. Moths and rust can destroy it. Thieves can steal it.
Instead, store up for yourselves treasure in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are good, then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes are weak. Then your whole body will be dark. And if the light within you is darkness, that means it is very dark!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Do not worry
I tell you, do not worry. Do not worry about your life and what you will eat or drink. And do not worry about your body and what you will wear. Is there nothing more important in life than eating? Are there not things more important for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the sky: they do not sow nor gather into barns, nor do they gather into storehouses. Your heavenly Father gives them, and are you not much better than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
Why are you interested in clothing? Look at how the wildflowers grow. They do not work, nor do they spin thread.
But that is what I am telling you. Not even Solomon in all his glory was arrayed like one of those lilies.
If God is thus clothed with the wild grass, does he not clothe you even better? For the grass is here today only, tomorrow it is thrown into the fire. Truly your faith is very weak!
Do not worry. And do not say, 'What will we eat?' or 'What will we drink?' or 'What will we wear?'
The people who do not believe in God are panting after all those things. Your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness will be given to you.
Therefore do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will have its own concerns. Each day has its own troubles.
Be just in your judgments of others.
Do not judge people, and you will not be judged.
You will be judged in the same way that you judge others. You will be measured in the same way that you measure others.
You look at a speck of sawdust in your friend's eye. But you don't notice the beam in your own eye.
How do you say to your friend: "Let me take a piece of wood out of your eye"? How do you say this while there is a piece of wood in your eye?
Hypocrite! First, take the plank out of your own eye; then you will see clearly to take the speck out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is God's to the dog. Do not throw pearls to the swine. Otherwise, swine may trample on it, and then turn and tear you apart. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and it will be given to you, seek and it will be found for you, knock and it will be opened to you.
Ask and it will be given to you; seek and you will find; knock and the door will be opened to you.
If your son asks for bread, which one of you will give him a stone?
Or does He ask you for a fee to give you a fish? So which of you would stand up to him with an authority, with a spell?
Even if you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father in heaven give good things to those who ask him!
As for those born into devotion, if they perform the obligations and avoid the forbidden, they will have the Garden of Eternity without accounting.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is easy that leads to destruction and to hell. Many go in by that gate.
But the door is small and the path is narrow, leading to life. Few find it. A tree and its fruits.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can tell what they really are by what they do. "Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?"
By the same token, every good tree bears good fruit, and every bad tree bears bad fruit.
The good tree only gives good, and the evil tree only gives evil.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
A tree is known by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not do many miracles in your name?'
Then I clearly say to them: "I never knew you. Depart from me, you evildoers!" The wise builder and the fool.
Everyone who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The sky is raining. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. But it is not falling. Because it is built on the rock.
But whoever hears my words and does not apply them is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
The sky is raining. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. And it falls with a loud noise.
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain. A great crowd followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. "Lord," he said, "if you are willing, you can make me 'clean'."
Jesus stretched out His hand and touched the man, and said, "I want to do this. Be 'clean'!" The man was immediately healed of his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not say this to anyone. Go, show yourself to the priest. And offer the gift that Moses commanded, for a testimony to them." Faith of a Roman Centurion
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
"Lord," he said, "my servant is at home and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The centurion said, "Lord, I am not worthy to have you enter under my roof. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed.
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to one, "Go," and he goes. And I say to another, "Come," and he comes. And I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was astonished and said to those who followed him, "I assure you, and it is the truth, that no one who has believed in Israel has a stronger faith than this woman."
I tell you, many will come from the east and the west, and will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven; and they will be seated with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob.
But whoever thinks he is one of her kingdom will be cast out into the darkness. There he will cry and grind his teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman soldiers, "Go! It will be as you have believed." And his servants were healed at that hour. Jesus heals many.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's wife. She was lying down on the bed. She had a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to serve him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus many who were possessed by demons. He cast out the spirits with a word, and he healed all the sick.
He did this so that what the prophet Isaiah said would come true. He said, "He has taken on what we should have taken on. He has carried on himself the diseases that we should have carried" (Isaiah 53:4). Following Jesus is expensive.
Jesus saw the crowd around him. He gave his disciples orders to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then one of the disciples came and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The dead have tombs, and the birds of the sky have nests. But as for the Son of Man, nowhere is there a place for him to lay his head."
Another disciple said to him, "Lord, first permit me to go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm.
Jesus entered a boat. His disciples followed him.
And suddenly a great storm of thunder and lightning came down on the lake, and the waves of the sea struck against the boat, but Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples got up and woke him. They said, "Lord, save us! We are perishing!"
And He said to him, "O you of little faith, why do you waver?" And Jesus arose and rebuked the winds and the sea, and there was a great calm in the sea.
And the disciples were astonished. And they asked, "Who then is this man? Even the winds and the sea obey him!" Jesus heals two demon-possessed men.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, into the region of Gerasenes. Two men possessed by demons met him. They had come from the tombs. The men were so savage that no one could pass that way.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried out. "Have you come here to punish us before the time when we are to be judged?"
Not very far away, there was a large herd of pigs gorging themselves.
The demons begged Jesus, saying, "If you cast us out, send us to the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the evil spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs, and the herd of pigs rushed down the steep bank into the lake, and into the deep water. Then they drowned.
So those who had been tending the pigs fled. They went into the city and told all this. And they reported to the people what had happened to the men who were possessed by demons.
Then he went out from all the city to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they requested him to do one thing, that he should come out from the Mount of Olives, which is where they are.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He walked across the water to the other side of the wilderness, and came to his own town.
Some men brought a man who could not walk to Jesus. He was lying on a mat. When Jesus saw that they had faith, he said to the man, "Take heart, son, your sins are forgiven."
Then one of the regular barbers from the legal side said, This fellow says something something very, very bad!
Jesus knew what was in their hearts. And he said, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Rise and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then He said to the man who could not walk, "Get up, take your bed and go to your house."
The man got up and went to his house.
When the crowd saw this, they were filled with awe. And they praised God for giving such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
And Jesus went on from there, and behold, a man named Matthew was sitting at the tax office. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And he rose and followed him.
Then Jesus entered the house of Matthew and was eating with him. And many tax collectors and sinners came and were eating with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "Those who are healthy do not need a physician, but those who are sick."
Go and learn what this means: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). "I did not come to gather those who think they are righteous with God to follow me. Rather, I came to gather sinners to follow me." They ask Jesus about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John came. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast, so why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered: How can the guests at my wedding feast be sad as long as I am still with them? The time will come when I will take the bridegroom away from them, and then they will be sad.
People do not patch an old garment with a piece of new cloth. For the new patch tears away from the old, making a greater tear.
New wine is not poured into old skins. If they do, the skins will burst. The wine will be spilled, and the skins will be ruined. No, new wine is poured into new skins. Both are saved." A dead girl and a suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying this, a leader of the synagogue came and knelt down before Jesus. "My daughter has just died," he said. "But come and put your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose and walked with him. His disciples also walked with him.
Suddenly a woman came from behind Jesus. She had been suffering from a disease that made her bleed. This disease had lasted for 12 years. She touched the tassel of his robe.
I thought, "All I have to do is touch his clothes. After that I will be healed."
Jesus turned and looked at her and said, "Woman, do not stop believing." She said to Him, "I have been healed by my faith." And the woman was healed at that hour.
When Jesus entered the house of the great man, he saw the musicians there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowd had been put outside, Jesus went in. And he took the girl by the hand, and said to her, "Talitha, qum!" which is translated, "Little girl, I say to you, arise!" And immediately the girl arose and walked, for she was twelve years old. And they were overcome with great amazement.
News of what Jesus had done spread throughout that region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And Jesus went from there, and followed by two blind men. And they cried out: "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus entered, the blind came to him. And he said to them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" They said, "Yes, Teacher."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "What you believed will happen to you."
They were able to see once again. Jesus sternly warned them, "Make sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it everywhere in that area.
And as they were coming out, another man was brought to Jesus. He was controlled by the devil, so he could not speak.
When the demon departed, the man spoke. The crowd was astonished and said, "Such a thing has never been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are few who are workers
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. And He taught in their synagogues. And He preached the gospel of the kingdom. And He healed every disease and every weakness.
When he saw the crowd, a deep compassion welled up within him for them. They were battered and bruised, helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few."
Ask the Lord of the Harvest to send workers into his harvest.
Jesus sends out the twelve disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to join him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease.
Here are the names of the twelve disciples. The first is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
Then Philip and Bartholomew, and John the Baptist, and Thomas, and Matthew the publican. Then James the son of Alphaeus, and Judas the Zealot.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Jude Iscariot. Jude is the one who will betray Jesus to his enemies later on.
Jesus sent out these twelve and gave them orders: "Do not go to Gentiles," he said. "Do not enter any Samaritan town."
Go to the children of Israel, for they are like sheep that have gone astray.
Go and teach people this message: 'The Kingdom of Heaven is near.'
Heal the sick. Raise the dead to life. Make the clean out of the unclean stars. Bring forth the devils. For you have taken freely, therefore give freely.
Do not carry gold, silver, or copper in belts with you.
Do not take a suitcase for traveling. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what he needs.
"When you enter a town or village, look for someone who is willing to welcome you. Stay at that person's home until you leave."
When you enter the house, greet the people who live there.
If that house welcomes you, give it your blessing with peace. Otherwise, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to what you say. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or city.
What I am going to tell you is true. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that town.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be shrewd as serpents and innocent as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
They will present you to rulers and kings because of you. You will bear witness against them and against non-Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. Then the right words will be given to you that you should say.
It will not be you who is speaking. But the spirit of your father is what will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver up brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver up their sons. Children will rise up against their parents and will kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But everyone who remains steadfast until the end will be immortalized.
"When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I tell you now is true. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes."
A student is no better than his teacher. A servant is no better than his master.
It suffices the student to be like his teacher. And it suffices the servant to be like his master. If the head of the house has been called Uzzir, what can the others living there expect?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be brought to light. And what is hidden will be revealed.
What I tell you in darkness, speak in the light. What is whispered in your ear, proclaim from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. Rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Aren't two pigeons sold for only one dinar? Yet not one of them falls to the ground without your father knowing about it.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Therefore, fear not. You are worth more than many sparrows.
What about the person who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to reverse "sons over their fathers. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. Mothers-in-law will be against their daughters-in-law.
'The enemies of a man are the members of his own household.' (Micah 7:6)
Whoever loves his father or mother more than me, does not deserve me. Whoever loves his son or daughter more than me, does not deserve me.
He who does not take his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. And if anyone loses his life because of me, he will find it.
He who welcomes you welcomes me, and he who welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
If someone receives a prophet as a prophet, that person will receive the reward of a prophet. And if someone receives a righteous person as a righteous person, that person will receive the reward of a righteous person.
If someone were to give even a cup of cold water to one of the little ones who are my followers, what I am about to tell you is true: that person will certainly be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples. Then he began to teach and preach in the villages of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come, or shall we look for another?"
Jesus answered, "Go and tell John what you hear and what you see.
It is seen by the blind. The disabled walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. And the good news brings joy to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not forbid what has been revealed to you.
And as John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. And he said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swayed by the wind?"
So what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. People who dress in fine clothes are in kings' palaces.
What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one about whom it was written in the book. It says: "'I send My messenger to prepare the way before you,' says the Lord, 'who stretches out the heavens, who lays the foundation of the earth, and who forms the spirit of man within him.' I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the Lord." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is greater than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, until now, the kingdom of heaven suffers violence, and the violent take it by force.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept it, John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let him who has ears hear.
What can I say about our people today? They are like children, sitting in the markets and calling out to people. They say,
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song. But you did not mourn.'
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, "He is possessed by a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, 'This man is a glutton; he is a friend of tax collectors and sinners.' But wisdom is justified by her deeds.' The cities that did not repent are therefore a warning to sinful nations.
Jesus began to rebuke the cities where he had performed most of his miracles, because the people had not repented of their sins. And he said,
How terrible this would be for you, Chorazin! How terrible this would be for you, Bethsaida! Imagine if the miracles that were performed in you were performed in Tyre and Sidon. They have long ago repented of their sins. They have put on black clothing. They have sat in ashes.
But I tell you this. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for you to enter the kingdom of heaven.
"What are you, Capernaum? Will you be exalted to heaven? No! But rather to Hell. For if the miracles that were done in you had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day."
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment it will be easier for Sodom than for you.
At that time Jesus said, "I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children."
Yes, Dad. This is what I wanted.
"My father has given me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to reveal him to."
Come to me, all of you who are weary and have carried heavy loads on your shoulders. I will give you rest.
Be my servants and learn from me, for I am gracious and do not assault. You will find comfort for your hearts.
My yoke is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Saturday, Jesus walked through the wheat fields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pull out some of the heads of wheat and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this. They said to Jesus: "Look, they are doing works that are not lawful to do on the Sabbath. And you are leading your disciples to do them!"
Jesus said, "Have you not read what David did? He and his men were hungry.
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. No one except the priests was allowed to eat it.
Have you not read the law? It tells us how the priests in the temple should work every Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I am telling you that here there is someone more important than the temple.
The Holy Book says: "I desire mercy, and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not have leveled accusations against the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
Then Jesus went out from there and entered the house of prayer.
There was a man who had a withered and bent hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they said to him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Would you not hold it and lift it out?"
Man is of greater value than sheep! Therefore, the law permits us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stand up and stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, as whole as the other.
But the Pharisees went out and plotted how they might kill Jesus. The chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew everything about the Pharisees' plans. So he left that place. Many followed Jesus, and all their sick were healed.
But he warned them not to tell who it was.
That what was said through the prophet Isaiah might be fulfilled. And he said,
This is my servant, whom I have chosen, upon whom my soul delights. I am well pleased with him. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will declare to the nations that the Lord makes all things good.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a twig that is already bent. He will not extinguish a flame that is already burning fiercely. He will fix everything.
"The nations shall put their hope in him" (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Beelzebul
A demon-possessed man was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and unable to speak. Jesus healed him. Then the man could speak and see.
The people were amazed and said, "Where is his father?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This man drives out demons by the power of Beelzebul, the ruler of demons."
Jesus knew what was in their minds. So he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself will be ruined, and every city or household divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom endure?
You say I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul. So by whose power do they cast them out? Let him therefore be judged.
But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and steal what he owns? You must first shackle him. Then you can rob his house.
Everyone who is not with me is against me. And everyone who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
So here is what I say. All sin and blasphemy will be forgiven, but blasphemy against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
He who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But he who speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come. And no one will speak a word against him now or in the days to come.
If you make the tree good, its fruit will be good. And if you make the tree bad, its fruit will be bad. You know the tree by its fruit.
You brood of vipers! How can you, being evil, speak any good thing? For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh.
The righteous man speaks a righteous word, this word is from the goodness that is in his heart. And the wicked man speaks a wicked word, this word is from the evil that is in his heart.
But here is what I say. On the day of reckoning, people will be held accountable for every careless word they spoke.
In your words, you condemn yourself or acquit yourself. The miraculous sign, Yunus.
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus. They said, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you in heaven."
He said, "Indeed, the disbelievers ask you to show them a sign! Say, 'I will show you a sign that is greater than the sign of Jonah.'"
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will be in the tomb three days and three nights.
The men of Nineveh will stand on the day of judgment with the people who are now living. And the men of Nineveh will prove to these people that they are guilty. The men of Nineveh turned away from their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now there is someone here who is more important than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will rise up on the day of judgment against the people who are living now. And she will condemn them. She came from a very great distance to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now here is someone who is greater than Solomon.
What happens when a jinn exits a person? They see on the earth but find nothing, so they return to them.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been cleaned and tidied.
Then the devil goes away and takes with him seven old men, more cunning than he is. They enter and dwell in it. And that man becomes more harmful than he was before. And thus he does to the wicked among the people of today. The mother of Jesus and his two brothers.
While Jesus was still speaking to the crowd, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told him, "Your mother and brothers are standing outside and want to speak with you."
Jesus answered and said, "Who is My mother and who are My brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "Here is my mother! Here are my brothers!"
"Whoever does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother and sister and mother."
The Farmer's Story
And in those hours Jesus went out from the house and sat by the sea of Galilee.
A great crowd gathered around him. So he entered a boat. And he sat in it. And all the people stood on the beach.
Then he told them many things using stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds."
Scattering the seeds on the ground. Some fell on a path. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and increased until they overshadowed the plants.
Other seed fell on good soil and produced crops that were a hundred, sixty or thirty times more than what was planted.
Let him who has ears hear.
The students came to him. They asked, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered: "You have been given the key to the knowledge of the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. This was not given to the repentant."
Everyone will be given more of that kind of knowledge. In fact, they will receive a lot. As for those who do not possess that kind of knowledge, even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Here's why I use stories when I talk to people. I say: "They look, but they don't really see. They listen, but they don't really hear or understand."
In it the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said: "You will indeed hear but never understand; you will indeed see but never perceive."
These people's hearts have become calloused; they barely hear with their ears, they have shut their eyes, and if perhaps they had seen with their eyes, and heard with their ears, and understood with their hearts, then perhaps they would have turned back to the Lord, and then he would have healed them. (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But God bless your eyes because they see. And God bless your ears because they hear.
What I am going to tell you is true. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message about the kingdom but do not understand it. Then the devil comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
Others received the seed that fell on rocky places. These are the ones who hear the message and receive it immediately with joy.
But they have no roots. So they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from the faith when troubles or suffering come because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. They are the ones who hear the message. But then the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth choke them. This prevents them from bearing fruit.
But others received the seed that fell on good soil. These are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a harvest 100, 60, or 30 times more than the farmer planted. The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowd. He said, "Thus is the kingdom of heaven. A man was sowing a field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The master's servants came to him. They said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did this thorny weeds come from?"
"He has been done in by an enemy," he answered. "The servants asked, 'Do you want us to go and remove the weeds?'"
"'No,' the owner replied. 'While you are pulling up the weeds, you might pull up the wheat along with them.'"
Let the two trees grow together until the harvest. Then I will tell the workers what to do. And these are the words that I will say to them. First gather the weeds. Bind them in bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my barn." "Stories of the Mustard Seed and the Leaven"
Jesus related another parable to the crowd of people. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and sowed it in a field.
It is the smallest of your seeds. But when it grows, it is greater than all the garden plants. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another parable, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, and it worked all through the dough."
Jesus spoke about all these things to the crowds using parables. He did not tell them anything without telling them a parable.
Jesus explains the parable of the tares.
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and went into the house. And His disciples came to Him, saying, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed is the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the devil.
An enemy sows them, that is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are pulled up and burned in the fire. It will be the same on the day of reckoning.
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather from his kingdom all causes of sin. And he will throw out the wicked.
They will cast them into the expansive Paradise, and they will blow into them from the fire, and they will devour them within it as a taste for them and a punishment.
Then the people of God will shine as brightly as the sun in the kingdom of their heavenly Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let them listen. The Parable of the Treasure and the Pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field, which when a man found, he hid it again, and was overjoyed. So he went and sold all that he had, and bought that field.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking a fine pearl.
He found a very precious one. So he went and sold everything he had. And he bought the pearl. The story of the catch.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. That was cast into the lake. And it gathered all kinds of fish.
When the fishermen had filled their boats, they drew them ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they cast the bad fish away.
Thus will the Day of Judgment be. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
They will cast the criminals into Hell, wherein they will go round, one above another. They will pass by them, angered. They will say, 'Oh, would that we could be sent back and not be among those who have been given the lie to the signs of their Lord and have become among the despairing ones.'
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes" they answered.
He said to them: "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a household who brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. He began to teach the people in their synagogue. And they were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom?" they said. "And where did he get this power to perform miracles?" they asked.
Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother called Mary? Are not his brothers called James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country. In his own country."
There, only a few miracles were made because they did not have faith.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
In those days, Herod, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has been raised from the dead! That is why he has such power to work miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias. She was the wife of his brother Philip.
John was telling Herod, "It is unlawful for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they thought that John was a prophet.
At Herod's birthday celebration, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He promised her that he would give her whatever she asked for.
Herodias's daughter said to her mother, "What shall I ask for?" The girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered a promise he had made to some dinner guests. So he asked one of his men to give her what she had asked for.
Herod cut off John's head in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went out to a deserted place in a boat. The crowd heard about it and they went out to follow him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came to the shore, he saw a large crowd. He was deeply concerned for them. He healed their sick.
As evening approached, the disciples came to him and said, "This is a desolate place, and it is now getting late. Send the crowds away, so they can go and buy food in the villages."
Jesus answered, "There is no need for them to go. Give them something to eat."
"We have only five loaves of bread and two fish," they answered.
"Bring them to me," he said.
Then Jesus, addressing the people, told them to sit on the grass. Taking the five loaves and the two fish, He looked up to heaven, blessed and broke the bread. The disciples gave it to the crowds. The people divided the food among themselves.
They all ate and were satisfied. The disciples picked up twelve baskets of fragments.
There were about 5,000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
When Jesus heard this, he ordered his disciples to embark on a ship and go to the region of Galilee, and that they should precede him, he and the crowds, to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the crowds away to their homes in Bethsaida.
After he sent them, he went up to a hill alone to pray, and when evening came he was there alone.
The boat was already far from land. It was being rocked by the waves because the winds were blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the water and were terrified. "It's a ghost!" they exclaimed, and screamed in fear.
Jesus immediately sent to them, saying, "Be of good cheer! I am he, do not be afraid."
"Lord, is it you?" Peter asked. "If it is, tell me to come to you on the water."
"Come," said Jesus. So Peter got out of the boat. He walked towards Jesus on the water.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was terrified and began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
And Jesus immediately stretched out his hand and grasped him, and said to him, "O you of little faith! Why did you doubt?"
When they boarded the boat, the wind calmed down.
Then he excommunicated those who were in the ark, praying to Christ, saying, "You are truly the Son of God." When Jesus looked up to heaven, he said, "O Jews, employ circumcision until you come to the children of Israel."
They crossed the lake and reached Galilee.
The men who lived there knew Jesus. So they sent a message everywhere nearby. And people brought all their sick to Jesus.
They were seeking from him that permission be given to the sick to only touch the hem of his garment. And he healed all who touched him.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked,
Why do your disciples not obey what the elders teach them? For your disciples do not wash their hands before they eat!"
Jesus answered, "Why do you not obey God's command? Instead, you want to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him only you shall serve." (Deuteronomy 6:13; Mark 12:29; Luke 10:27; Matthew 4:10) He also said, "You shall not misuse the name of the Lord your God, for the Lord will not hold anyone guiltless who misuses His name." (Exodus 20:7; Leviticus 5:1; 1 Kings 21:10-11; Ezekiel 8:9-11; Matthew 26:61; Mark 14:58; Luke 22:64; John 18:40; Acts 3:13-15; 4:10-12; 5:40-42; 26:10-11; Romans 3:4; 1 Timothy 1:13; Hebrews 6:5; 10:28-29; 1 John 2:4; Jude 1:8-10)
But you satisfy people by ordering them to do some of what you command them to do, and you hate it, and you order them to strike their footsteps on what they did not assume, and you order them to lend God, He has made them witnesses over themselves, wrongdoers.
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God void for the sake of your traditions.
O ye hypocrites! Isaiah was indeed righteous when he prophesied about you, saying:
"These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me."
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only human rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd and said, "Listen and understand."
What enters the mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of the mouth, that is what defiles you.
Then the disciples came to him. And they said, Do you not know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?
Jesus answered, "There are trees that my Father who is in heaven did not plant."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If the blind leads the blind, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Have you not yet understood?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But that which proceeds out of the mouth, this defileth the man. It is these very things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. These give rise to murder, adultery, and other sexual sins. They also lead to theft, false testimony, and lying to others.
This is what makes you "unclean". But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean". The faith of a Canaanite woman.
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a woman of Canaan who lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to Him and cried out, saying, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! For my daughter is severely possessed by a demon."
Jesus did not say a word. His disciples came to him. And they asked him, "Send her away from us, for she follows us and troubles us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman knelt before him, saying, "Oh Lord! Help me!"
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to their dog."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Then Jesus said: "Woman, you have great faith. Let it be done for you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that very hour. Jesus feeds four thousand people.
Jesus left there. He made his way along the lake of Galilee. Then he went up to a hill and sat down.
Large crowds gathered around him. They brought the blind and those who could not walk. They also brought the lame and those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them before him, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The sick were healed. Those who could not walk were able to walk. The blind were given sight. Therefore, the eyes of the Israelites shone with grace.
Then Jesus destroyed his disciples, so that they would come to him. He said, "What saddens me is what they are suffering. They have eaten with me for three days and one night. And they have no food. And I do not want to send them away fasting, lest they faint on the way. For some of them came from far away."
His disciples said to him, "There is nothing here." And they said, "From where should we buy bread to satisfy these people?"
"How much bread do you have?" Jesus asked. They answered, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus ordered the crowd to sit on the ground.
He took the seven loaves and the fish, thanked them, then broke them and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave it to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. Afterward, the disciples picked up seven baskets of leftover fragments.
There were 4000 men who ate. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowd away, he went up into the boat. Then he went into the region near Magadan.
They ask Jesus for a sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He replied, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'The weather will be fine.' The sky is red."
And in the morning she says, "It will rain today. The sky is red and overcast." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
Indeed, a bad and treacherous people are searching for a miraculous sign. But they will not be given any sign except the sign of Jonah. Then their Jesus left and departed. The assembly of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked among themselves about this. And they said: "He must be saying this because we did not take bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said to them, "O little faith! Why do you reason among yourselves that you have no bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets of fragments you collected?
Do you forget the seven loaves for the two thousand? Do you forget how many baskets of fragments you gathered?
How is it that you do not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus had not told them to beware of the yeast of bread. Rather, He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. And he asked his disciples there, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They said, "Some say it is John the Baptist. Others say it is Job. Still others say it is Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus said, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For it was not by any human hand that this has come about. Rather, my Father who is in heaven."
This is what I tell you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then He revealed to His disciples, "Do not tell anyone about Me," for He is the Christ of God. That is Jesus, the son of Mary. Say, "I am a witness,"
From that time, Jesus began to teach his disciples what was going to happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There he must suffer many things at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed and on the third day rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "No, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. For it is not with your mind on the things of God. But your mind is on the things of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, 'If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow me.'
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What is the use of gaining the whole world and losing one's own soul? Or what can anyone give in exchange for their own self?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father, and with Him will come His angels. And He will reward each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is true. Some of those standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Change Jesus' appearance.
Six days later, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, the brother of James, and led them up a high mountain. There they were all alone.
Before them, his appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Christ, "Master, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will erect three tents. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. And a voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to Him!"
And when the disciples heard it, they were exceedingly sorry. And they placed their faces to the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them. And He said, "Arise. Do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They went down the mountain. And as they were coming down, Jesus said to them, "Do not tell anyone anything about what you have seen, until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is necessary. It is necessary that Elijah appear and renew all things."
But I tell you that Elijah has come. They did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. Likewise, they will make the Son of Man suffer.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus feeds one thousand young men
As they came near the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus. He fell on his knees before Jesus.
"Oh Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son, he is trembling violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him a number of your students. But they were unable to heal him.
"O you wicked and evil men!" Jesus answered. "How long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you?"
Jesus commanded the devil to come out of the boy, and he came out. And he was healed in that hour.
Then the disciples came, seeking to have Jesus release him. They said, "Why could we not we ourselves come out and bring him?"
He said to him, "Because your faith is very small. What I am about to tell you is true. If your faith is like a mustard seed, it will be enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." At this, the disciples were filled with sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax
Jesus and His disciples arrived in Capernaum. The tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does," he answered. And when Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "From whom do the kings of the earth receive tolls and taxes? From their children or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children ought not to pay," Jesus said to him.
But we do not want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your fishing hook. Take the very first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find exactly the coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tribute for me and for you.
